//? Title: Adversarial Autoencoders: Deterministic Codes and an Aggregate Prior
//? Subtitle: Three explicit phases, one reconstruction gradient snapshot and four independent Adam states
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
An adversarial autoencoder learns to reconstruct observations while asking a latent discriminator
to distinguish encoded data from a supplied prior. This report separates deterministic encoding,
Bernoulli reconstruction and aggregate-prior adversarial objectives, then executes the original
small tanh model on an exhaustive294-image circle/square lattice. Its three parameter groups
use four independent Adam states, with encoder/decoder reconstruction gradients computed before
either group is changed. After21 minibatches and84 optimizer updates, validation selects epoch3;
test pixel MSE0.14893 is worse than the fitting-only mean0.04855. All weak reconstructions and
unfiltered prior-decoder probabilities remain visible. Finite adversarial losses do not certify
aggregate matching, disentanglement, an ELBO or useful generation.
)
\toc

> Three networks and three different objectives

[Makhzani, Shlens, Jaitly, Goodfellow and Frey](https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.05644) introduce
adversarial matching of an autoencoder's aggregated code distribution to a chosen prior.
This report retains the migrated deterministic teaching configuration: E:784–16tanh–4linear,
G:4–16tanh–784linear logits, D:4–16tanh–1linear logit. Sigmoid decoder values are Bernoulli
probabilities; there is no posterior mean/variance pair, posterior epsilon or per-example
Gaussian KL. Prior draws are real discriminator examples and never enter E or G reconstruction.

For image rows X, supplied prior rows U, code Z=E(X), logits L=G(Z) and discriminator logits
s_r=D(U),s_f=D(Z), the three losses are

$$
L_{\rm rec}=\frac1B\sum_{b,j}[\operatorname{softplus}(L_{bj})-X_{bj}L_{bj}],\\
L_D=\frac1P\sum_p\operatorname{softplus}(-s_{r,p})
+\frac1B\sum_b\operatorname{softplus}(s_{f,b}),\qquad
L_{E,\rm adv}=\frac1B\sum_b\operatorname{softplus}(-s_{f,b}).
$$
\alttext(Reconstruction sums Bernoulli pixel NLL and averages images. The discriminator labels prior real and encoded data fake. The encoder adversarial objective assigns its encoded data the real label.)

The E adversarial term is non-saturating: it minimizes a real-label loss rather than maximizing
D's fake-label loss. The core returns all mathematical paths without implicit detaches; the
caller chooses which parameter group receives an update. Encoder gradients of D loss can
exist mathematically even though the discriminator phase updates only D.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/aae_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
